24. { -KEY- } h4 $1 { Attacking the king is logical with Black's queen on the other wing. } Ba6 { White has a good choice, but the exercise ends here. There's no need to decide on your next move before executing 24.h4. } 25. Bf3 ( 25. Bxa6 Qxa6 26. Qf6 $1 ( { ( } 26. h5 { runs into the triple threat } Qe2 { .) } ) 
 ( 26. Bf3 h5 { The only way to avoid h4-h5. } ( { ( } 26. Bf3 Qe2 27. Rd7 Qxe4 28. h5 $18 { ) } ) 
27. Rd7 { Threatening Rg5xg6 with mate to follow. } Ra7 28. Rd2 $1 { The rook on a7 can't challenge White on the c-file. The threat is g2-g4. } Qb7 29. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 30. Qg5 Kh7 31. Rd3 Qxe4 32. Qxh5 Kg7 33. Rg3 Qg6 34. Qc5 Qxg3 35. fxg3 $18 { White advances the kingside pawns. This line was, of course, far too long for anyone to calculate. } ) 
 ) 
f6 ( 25... Rac8 { is met by: } 26. Rxc8 Rxc8 27. h5 $18 ) 
26. Qe3 Rad8 27. Rxd8 Rxd8 { A position from The Woodpecker Method. There is a tactical win. } 28. e5 $1 f5 29. Rc8 $1 { Black resigned. } 
 1-0

